<?xml version="1.0"?>
<model>
  <name>fpv_quad_enemy</name>
  <version>1.0</version>
  <sdf version="1.9">model.sdf</sdf>
  <author>
    <name>Interceptor Simulation -- red enemy quad, proper 3D, vendored x500 meshes</name>
    <email>emersonium2@gmail.com</email>
  </author>
  <description>
    Replaces the flat-billboard target: models/fpv_target_markerless (and its
    predecessors fpv_target / apriltag_target) laid their 4 arms/props out in
    the local Y-Z plane, i.e. a vertical plane facing -X -- a billboard, not a
    real quad, that breaks the moment the mover yaws/banks/pitches it. This
    model is a genuine 3D quadcopter: it vendors the real x500 airframe
    meshes (NXP-HGD-CF.dae frame, 5010Base.dae motor bases, 5010Bell.dae
    motor bells, 1345_prop_ccw.stl / 1345_prop_cw.stl props, copied with
    cp -L from this repo's models/x500_base so they are real files, not
    symlinks) and flattens x500_base's 4 rotor links (each a separate link
    with its own joint, needed only so PX4 can spin real props) into ONE
    static "base" link's visuals, since this target is teleported kinematically
    by scripts/m4_target_mover.py and never spins anything. The rotor plane
    sits in local X-Y at z~0.028-0.044 -- HORIZONTAL -- exactly like a real
    flying quad, forward = +X (matches x500_base's NXP_FMUK66_FRONT decal
    convention). The 4 props and 4 motor bells carry a red "enemy" material
    accent so it reads as a hostile target, distinct from the interceptor's
    own (dark carbon / blue-accented) x500 reskin in models/x500_base.

    Static, no collision, no inertial, no joints, no plugins -- 13 visuals
    total (1 frame + 4 motor bases + 4 props + 4 bells). Does NOT touch or
    replace models/fpv_target_markerless/ (kept as-is; this is a new,
    separate model). Validated with `gz sdf -k` (static SDF check) only --
    no sim boot performed for this deliverable.
  </description>
</model>
